From: Alex Scotti <alexscotti@gmail.com>
Subject: vdec: bound the wait for the DMC channel to go idle

dec_dmc_port_ctrl() turns a decoder's memory requests off and then spins until
the memory controller reports the channel idle, with no timeout. On S6 (no
VDEC bus-control block) every H.264 run goes through it: run() ->
vdec_reset_core() -> dec_pipeline_idle_ctrl(VLD, 0) -> dec_dmc_port_ctrl(),
on the vdec-core thread at SCHED_FIFO priority.

An AM9 Pro (S6) playing A.I. Artificial Intelligence was found with its
H.264 decoder timed out on the film's first frame (pc 0x149, status 0xf0 =
still searching for a header, input level not moving), vdec-core then
RT-throttled every second for as long as the box stayed up, and the decoder
instance stuck ACTIVE ("vdec_disconnect timeout!!! status: 0x3"). Kodi could
not close the decoder, the box hung in shutdown, and only cutting AC brought
it back. A run that never returns is what keeps an instance ACTIVE; this is
the unbounded wait on that run path.

Bound it at 100ms (a healthy channel goes idle in microseconds), log the DMC
request and status words when it expires, and carry on so the core reset that
follows can recover the decoder.

---
--- a/drivers/frame_provider/decoder/utils/vdec.c
+++ b/drivers/frame_provider/decoder/utils/vdec.c
@@ -939,6 +939,7 @@
 static void dec_dmc_port_ctrl(bool dmc_on, u32 target)
 {
 	unsigned long flags;
+	unsigned long timeout;
 	unsigned int sts_reg_addr = DMC_CHAN_STS;
 	unsigned int mask = 0;
 	unsigned int cpu_type = get_cpu_major_id();
@@ -1028,9 +1029,27 @@
 			sts_reg_addr = DMC_CHAN_STS;
 			break;
 		}
-		while (!(codec_dmcbus_read(sts_reg_addr)
-				& mask))
-				;
+		/*
+		 * Bounded: the decoder core thread calls this at RT priority at the
+		 * start of every run (vdec_reset_core). If the channel never reports
+		 * idle - a decode that stalled with its memory requests in flight -
+		 * an unbounded wait spins that thread forever: no decoder can run or
+		 * close again, the player blocks in the driver, and the box needs a
+		 * power cycle. An S6 (AM9 Pro) was found that way - vdec-core
+		 * RT-throttled, its vdec stuck ACTIVE - right after an H.264
+		 * first-frame decode timeout; this is the unbounded wait on the run
+		 * path for S6's VLD. Go on and let the reset that follows recover.
+		 */
+		timeout = jiffies + HZ / 10;
+		while (!(codec_dmcbus_read(sts_reg_addr) & mask)) {
+			if (time_after(jiffies, timeout)) {
+				pr_err("%s: dmc channel idle timeout, target %d req 0x%x sts 0x%x mask 0x%x\n",
+					__func__, target,
+					codec_dmcbus_read(DMC_REQ_CTRL),
+					codec_dmcbus_read(sts_reg_addr), mask);
+				break;
+			}
+		}
 	}
 }
 
